WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.680
Jupiter Ascending led to one&nbsp;
star's reputation descending.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:07.855
How did these actors win both&nbsp;
Hollywood's highest and lowest honors?

00:00:08.101 --> 00:00:13.040
Al Pacino could've justifiably won an Oscar&nbsp;
for any number of his memorable performances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.080
He's been nominated nine times, for the likes of&nbsp;
The Godfather, Dog Day Afternoon, and Glengarry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.400
Glen Ross. But it was his performance as a blind&nbsp;
and depressed former Army colonel in 1992's Scent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.360
of a Woman that netted him his win. It may not&nbsp;
be remembered as the classic that some of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.301
other films are, but it did give us a killer&nbsp;
tango scene and an unforgettable catchphrase.

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:35.567
"Hoo-ah!"

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.920
Pacino has a knack for playing&nbsp;
likable bad guys, crooked good guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.120
and big personalities, including the Devil&nbsp;
himself. He's also dipped a toe into self-parody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.440
especially later in his career. He does,&nbsp;
after all, have a very specific persona.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.960
But he may have taken things just a little too&nbsp;
far with 2011's Jack and Jill. The critically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.480
reviled Adam Sandler comedy stars the SNL&nbsp;
alum as the titular twin brother and sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.440
and Pacino as the man who is inexplicably&nbsp;
smitten with Jill. Jack and Jill became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.400
first movie ever to sweep the Razzies, which&nbsp;
included Al Pacino as Worst Supporting Actor.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.280
"It's not Al anymore. It's Dunk!"

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.720
"Dunkaccino?"

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.320
"Don't mind if I do!"

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.560
At the height of her fame, Kim Basinger&nbsp;
was one of Hollywood's go-to icy blondes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.640
It was exactly that stereotype that she&nbsp;
deconstructed with her performance as Lynn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.880
Bracken in 1997's L.A. Confidential, for which&nbsp;
she won a well-deserved Supporting Actress Oscar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.680
Lynn is a high-end call girl in 1950s Los&nbsp;
Angeles who has gone under the knife and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.840
dyed her hair in order to resemble&nbsp;
real-life movie star Veronica Lake.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.080
After Basinger's Oscar win, she continued to&nbsp;
work, but mostly in movies that didn't match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:50.400
her full talents. By 2017, she was playing second&nbsp;
fiddle to Dakota Johnson in Fifty Shades Darker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.600
the second installment in the Fifty Shades of&nbsp;
Grey trilogy. She played Elena Lincoln, a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.520
businesswoman who grooms a teenage Christian Grey&nbsp;
and turns him on to BDSM. Inexplicably, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.160
still on good terms years later. The role didn't&nbsp;
exactly return Basinger to her former glory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.144
as she ended up with a Razzie and reviews&nbsp;
that compared her to a waxwork animatronic.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.480
1997's Good Will Hunting wasn't Ben Affleck's&nbsp;
first movie, but it was the one that cemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.400
his status as a major talent both&nbsp;
in front of and behind the camera.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:26.256
He won the Oscar for Original Screenplay along&nbsp;
with his buddy Matt Damon at the tender age of 25.

00:02:26.256 --> 00:02:27.458
"How you like me now?!"

00:02:27.458 --> 00:02:30.640
Affleck went on to win another Oscar,&nbsp;
this time for Best Picture as one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.120
the producers of 2012's Argo. He's&nbsp;
never been nominated for his acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.200
though, despite turning in plenty of&nbsp;
praiseworthy performances over the years.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.480
But Affleck doesn't exactly have a perfect&nbsp;
track record. 2003's Gigli was basically one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.040
big punchline when it was released at the peak&nbsp;
of his and Jennifer Lopez's original tabloid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.440
superstardom. Though the crime comedy about&nbsp;
a hitman and his hot babysitter starred two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.880
of the most famous people in the world, it bombed&nbsp;
hard with audiences and critics. Affleck ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:05.280
with a couple of Razzies, for Worst Actor and as&nbsp;
one half of Worst Screen Couple alongside Lopez.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.160
There haven't been too many actors who have&nbsp;
won Oscars for foreign language films. In 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.680
Italian filmmaker Roberto Benigni became one of&nbsp;
them when he nabbed the Lead Actor statue over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.240
Ian McKellen, Tom Hanks, Edward Norton,&nbsp;
and Nick Nolte for Life is Beautiful.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.680
With its high concept narrative&nbsp;
and mannered performance style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.000
Life Is Beautiful isn't for everybody.&nbsp;
But if it is to your taste, then Benigni&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.480
is undeniably the film's heart and soul.&nbsp;
A few years later, he tried to apply that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.520
same sense of dark whimsy to the classic&nbsp;
story of Pinocchio, to disastrous results.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:42.240
Pinocchio was nominated for six Razzies&nbsp;
in total, and a 50-year-old Benigni was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.200
named Worst Actor for his portrayal of the&nbsp;
little-boy puppet. Bizarrely enough, he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.811
go on to appear in another Pinocchio movie in&nbsp;
2019; although this time, he played Geppetto.

00:03:52.935 --> 00:03:57.280
Halle Berry began her career as a pageant&nbsp;
queen and model with the kind of otherworldly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.680
beauty that can be a double-edged sword.&nbsp;
It surely helped Berry break into showbiz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.440
but it also meant she had to fight harder&nbsp;
to be considered a serious thespian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.720
She reached the pinnacle of her career with her&nbsp;
Lead Actress Oscar win for 2001's Monster's Ball,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.040
making her the first Black actress ever&nbsp;
to win in that category. Her performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.145
as a woman whose husband is on death row is&nbsp;
tortured, raw, and totally free of vanity.

00:04:20.292 --> 00:04:24.880
"Black man in America, you&nbsp;
can't be like that! I'm tryna-"

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.560
When Berry was cast as the ferocious antihero&nbsp;
in 2004's Catwoman, it was clearly an effort to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.920
capitalize on her Oscar win as well as her sex&nbsp;
appeal. Alas, the embarrassing final product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:41.760
was met with hisses and failed to recoup its big&nbsp;
budget. Catwoman led the pack of bad movies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.120
year with seven Razzie nominations and four wins.&nbsp;
Though critics were generally kinder to Berry than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.920
they were to the movie itself, she still took home&nbsp;
the Razzie for Worst Actress. Always a good sport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:56.420
she showed up in person to claim her&nbsp;
trophy and even gave a good-natured speech.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.680
Marlon Brando was an icon of the silver screen&nbsp;
who brought method acting into the mainstream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.400
For his efforts, he was rewarded with two&nbsp;
Oscars. The first came in the heyday of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.080
stardom when he won Lead Actor for 1954's On the&nbsp;
Waterfront, in which he plays a once-promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.960
boxer turned dockworker who gets involved in&nbsp;
the criminal dealings of a corrupt union boss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.720
18 years later, he delivered his&nbsp;
best-remembered performance as mob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:26.240
boss Don Vito Corleone in The Godfather, and&nbsp;
he was rewarded with another Lead Actor trophy.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.920
Brando was Oscar-nominated six&nbsp;
other times throughout his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.720
but his filmography is more&nbsp;
checkered than you might realize.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.000
A particular low point came when he won the&nbsp;
Worst Supporting Actor Razzie for the 1996&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.600
remake of The Island of Dr. Moreau. He plays the&nbsp;
title character in a film that was resoundingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.920
rejected by audiences and critics. The&nbsp;
project was a disaster from the start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.240
with Brando experiencing a number of personal&nbsp;
setbacks, including the death of his daughter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.032
He was reportedly so checked out that he ended up&nbsp;
having his lines fed to him through an earpiece.

00:05:58.032 --> 00:06:02.560
Sandra Bullock is one of the most popular&nbsp;
and versatile actresses in the industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.320
She projects confidence as well&nbsp;
as humility as she effortlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:11.680
transitions from drama to comedy to action. In&nbsp;
fact, she has so much range that she managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.280
win the most high-profile awards for both&nbsp;
best AND worst actress in the same year.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.320
In 2009, she starred in both The Blind&nbsp;
Side and All About Steve. The former is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.240
a based-on-a-true-story sports drama&nbsp;
in which she plays Leigh Anne Tuohy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.960
the woman who adopts Michael Oher, a foster&nbsp;
kid who goes on to play professional football.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.560
It was a huge box office hit, and&nbsp;
Oscar voters awarded it with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.920
Best Picture nomination and a&nbsp;
Lead Actress win for Bullock.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.560
However, the day before the Oscars, Bullock&nbsp;
attended that year's Razzies to accept her award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.560
for Worst Actress for All About Steve. As a&nbsp;
gag, she gave out DVD copies of that movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.600
in which she plays Mary, a woman whose entire&nbsp;
personality basically consists of a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:59.040
of twee quirks thrown into a blender. She's a&nbsp;
crossword puzzle writer with a pet hamster and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.080
shiny red boots who becomes obsessed with a man&nbsp;
she meets on a blind date and decides to stalk him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.760
after he tries to let her down easy. So, not&nbsp;
exactly the most charming part she's ever played.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.160
After years as a leading man, Kevin Costner&nbsp;
decided to try directing. His debut effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.200
1990's Dances with Wolves, was received&nbsp;
extremely well. It impressed critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:25.760
made hundreds of millions of dollars at the&nbsp;
box office, revitalized the Western genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.880
and earned a whopping 12 Oscar nominations,&nbsp;
including three for Costner as producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:36.240
director, and lead actor. He won for Picture&nbsp;
and Director, as Dances legitimized him as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.720
artist behind the camera and earned him&nbsp;
a blank check to make his next movie.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:46.000
That next movie was 1997's The Postman, which&nbsp;
initially didn't seem too far removed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.840
Dances with Wolves. It's a sweeping neo-Western&nbsp;
directed by and starring Costner, but this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:57.200
the drama takes place in an alternate,&nbsp;
post-apocalyptic 2013 after a plague has wiped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.160
out much of the human population. Costner plays&nbsp;
the title character, who's not a mail deliverer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.760
but a migrant who stumbled upon an old uniform&nbsp;
and is endeavoring to restore civilization through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.720
the re-establishment of a postal service.&nbsp;
Critics and audiences returned it to sender,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.560
and Costner took home the "prizes"&nbsp;
for Worst Picture, Worst Director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:22.000
and Worst Actor. Those aren't his only career&nbsp;
Razzies either, as he'd previously won Worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.962
Remake or Sequel for Wyatt Earp and Worst Actor&nbsp;
for both Earp and Robin Hood: Prince of Thieves.

00:08:27.962 --> 00:08:33.520
Leonardo DiCaprio was a child star who seamlessly&nbsp;
transitioned to one of the biggest names in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:39.040
industry as an adult. Over the years, he's starred&nbsp;
in more than his fair share of Oscar-nominated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.080
Oscar-winning movies. Some of them even won Best&nbsp;
Picture, but it took a while before he took home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:49.120
a trophy himself for one of his performances.&nbsp;
That finally happened when he won Lead Actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:55.040
for 2015's The Revenant, in which he plays a&nbsp;
frontiersman who's brutally mauled by a bear.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:00.080
Leo's Razzie arrived nearly 20 years&nbsp;
earlier. In 1998's The Man in the Iron Mask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:05.440
he plays two roles: France's King Louis XIV&nbsp;
and his secret identical twin brother Philippe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.840
the titular Man in the Iron Mask.&nbsp;
Though the film received mixed reviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:15.280
audiences still bought tickets in part because&nbsp;
of the Leo fever that was in full swing in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.920
wake of Titanic's mammoth success. Razzie&nbsp;
voters weren't so enamored, though. Playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:21.280
both the hero and the vaillain sounds&nbsp;
like fodder for awards' attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.480
but the only trophy bestowed upon him was&nbsp;
the Golden Raspberry for Worst Screen Couple.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:33.920
"My twin. My blood. A fact which&nbsp;
has kept him alive until now."

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.640
Faye Dunaway began her career on Broadway&nbsp;
in the 1960s, and by the end of the decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.360
she was playing one half of the outlaw duo&nbsp;
in Bonnie and Clyde. She received her first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.440
Oscar nomination for that role, and her second&nbsp;
for 1974's mystery noir Chinatown. The third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.640
time was ultimately the charm when she took&nbsp;
home the trophy for 1976's Network, an iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.640 --> 00:10:00.320
satire about a TV channel's sagging ratings and a&nbsp;
messiah-like figure who resurrects them. Dunaway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:05.360
plays the network's head of programming who has&nbsp;
few, if any, personal or professional scruples.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.000
Just five years later, Dunaway starred in a&nbsp;
film that generated significant controversy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:16.400
as she played screen legend Joan Crawford in the&nbsp;
1981 biopic Mommie Dearest. The movie focuses on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:21.600
Crawford's adoption and subsequent abuse of her&nbsp;
daughter Christina. Dunaway's performance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.320
so over-the-top that Mommie Dearest was&nbsp;
eventually embraced as a camp classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:30.160
But that ironic appreciation didn't&nbsp;
prevent it from collecting five Razzies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.722
including Dunaway's for Worst Actress.

00:10:32.722 --> 00:10:37.611
"No wire hangers!"

00:10:37.611 --> 00:10:41.360
To win his pair of Oscars, Mel Gibson&nbsp;
followed a similar path to Kevin Costner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.160
as he directed and starred in a rousing&nbsp;
historical epic. 1995's Braveheart ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.000
up winning Best Picture, and Gibson was&nbsp;
named Best Director in a bit of a surprise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:55.920
Braveheart earned praise for its battle scenes&nbsp;
but was dinged for its historical accuracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.880
and audiences were split over&nbsp;
its use of graphic violence.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:03.840
After a bit of an involuntary break due&nbsp;
to some racist and anti-Semitic remarks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:08.240
Gibson began to appear in movies again&nbsp;
semi-regularly in the 2010s. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:14.080
he never quite regained his heavyweight status.&nbsp;
In 2017's Daddy's Home 2, he plays father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:18.720
to Mark Wahlberg as a fighter pilot and&nbsp;
astronaut whose regressive masculinity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:24.480
neglect made his son into a problematic man.&nbsp;
Alas, casting Gibson as an outdated archetype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:29.520
ultimately came across as more cringe-worthy than&nbsp;
self-aware. Unlike his Oscars for Braveheart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.215
his Worst Supporting Actor Razzie for&nbsp;
Daddy's Home 2 wasn't exactly a surprise.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:39.040
Over Charlton Heston's 84 years, he went from&nbsp;
being a vocal supporter of the civil rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.880
movement to a conservative NRA activist. At the&nbsp;
same time, he also went from a stoic leading man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:50.240
in action epics to someone who mostly did ironic&nbsp;
cameos and voiceover work in kids' movies.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:53.760
Heston starred in a number of iconic&nbsp;
movies over the course of his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.200
and he won his Oscar for one of them.&nbsp;
That was as the title character in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.240
1959 religious epic Ben-Hur, even though&nbsp;
his performance was somewhat overshadowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.520
by the film's breathtaking and&nbsp;
then-cutting edge chariot race.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.160
Nearly half a century later, Heston won&nbsp;
Worst Supporting Actor at the 2002 Golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:15.280
Raspberries for his appearances in three separate&nbsp;
movies. Those roles were an English Mastiff in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:19.680
Cats &amp; Dogs, a heavily-made up Zaius in&nbsp;
Tim Burton's Planet of the Apes remake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:24.240
and a parody of own his gun-toting persona&nbsp;
in the romantic comedy Town &amp; Country.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:29.520
Jared Leto became a household name in 1994&nbsp;
as the swoon-worthy Jordan Catalano on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:34.800
canceled-too-soon teen drama My So-Called Life.&nbsp;
He soon made the transition to the big screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:40.400
and he eventually got some Academy love when he&nbsp;
won Best Supporting Actor for his role in 2013's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.720
Dallas Buyers Club as an HIV-positive&nbsp;
trans woman struggling with addiction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.280
His performance was widely&nbsp;
praised, although many felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:51.200
that a trans actor should've been given&nbsp;
the opportunity to play the part instead.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:56.480
In the years since, Leto has taken on bigger and&nbsp;
bolder roles. This trend reached its apex with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:01.680
2021's House of Gucci, in which he plays the&nbsp;
fashion family's black sheep Paolo underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:08.240
pounds of prosthetics and makeup. Such flamboyant&nbsp;
choices came with great risk: his efforts were so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:13.840
divisive that he seemed to have as much a shot&nbsp;
at being nominated for another Oscar as he did a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:19.360
Razzie. He actually landed a nomination from the&nbsp;
Screen Actors Guild and several critics groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:24.517
though the Academy passed him by, and the Golden&nbsp;
Raspberries declared him Worst Supporting Actor.

00:13:24.517 --> 00:13:29.200
"The tiny little mouse is a character. Who knew?"

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:34.000
Younger audiences probably know Liza Minnelli&nbsp;
best for her hilarious guest appearances as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.160
Lucille 2 on Arrested Development.&nbsp;
As the daughter of Judy Garland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:41.200
Minnelli managed to carve out&nbsp;
an impressive career for herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:45.680
outside of her mother's shadow. That includes&nbsp;
an Academy Award-winning performance as Sally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:50.960
Bowles in 1972's Cabaret , the classic&nbsp;
musical about pre-World War II Germany.

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:56.240
After the 1981 rom-com Arthur, Minnelli had&nbsp;
a harder time securing the kinds of juicy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:01.920
triple-threat roles in which she so easily&nbsp;
shined. In 1988, she appeared in two poorly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:07.200
reviewed films that together earned her a Razzie&nbsp;
for Worst Actress. In Arthur 2: On the Rocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:12.560
she reprises her role as Linda, Arthur's now-wife&nbsp;
who wants to adopt a child with her supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:17.680
charming alcoholic husband. But it was probably&nbsp;
her role as a sex worker with a heart of gold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:22.320
in the Burt Reynolds-starring Rent-a-Cop that&nbsp;
really earned her the Golden Raspberry. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:27.360
received abysmal reviews from critics and the few&nbsp;
members of the public who saw it. It made a measly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:33.360
$300,000 dollars at the box office, which didn't&nbsp;
come anywhere close to its $16 million budget.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:38.400
After getting his start on the London stage&nbsp;
and filling small roles in period piece films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:43.040
Eddie Redmayne quickly became a leading man&nbsp;
and a critical darling. He won an Oscar for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:48.640
his portrayal of famed cosmologist Stephen&nbsp;
Hawking in 2014's The Theory of Everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:52.960
In becoming Hawking, Redmayne had to avoid&nbsp;
making it seem as though he was poking fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:57.840
at the late scientist's disabilities. By most&nbsp;
critics' and voters' assessments, he succeeded.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:03.440
Just one year later, Redmayne attempted something&nbsp;
completely different. In the Wachowski sibling's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.680
2015 trippy space opera Jupiter Ascending, he&nbsp;
plays the villain, Balem Abrasax, an alien rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:15.200
kid capitalist. It's sometimes said that acting&nbsp;
is about making choices, and boy does Redmayne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:20.720
make some choices here. Chief among them is the&nbsp;
voice that he puts on. As he explained to GQ,

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:23.760
"My character had had his larynx&nbsp;
ripped out by this wolf man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.634
and so I made the slightly bold choice, which&nbsp;
I thought was right, of talking like this."

00:15:28.634 --> 00:15:31.760
"I loved my mother."

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:35.440
Unfortunately, winning the Razzie&nbsp;
for Worst Supporting Actor that year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:40.000
convinced Redmayne that his performance in&nbsp;
Jupiter Ascending wasn't very good. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:45.246
fans of his have reclaimed his bold acting&nbsp;
as a brilliant stroke of artistic expression.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:49.520
Sir Laurence Olivier's name is&nbsp;
synonymous with the craft of acting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:54.240
Even those who are only passingly familiar with&nbsp;
him could easily guess that he won an Oscar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:59.360
though they might not be able to guess for what&nbsp;
film. The master thespian was nominated for 11&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:03.680
acting Oscars over the course of his career.&nbsp;
He won a couple of honorary Academy Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:07.840
as well as a competitive trophy&nbsp;
for Lead Actor in 1948's Hamlet.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:13.200
Like all famous actors, Olivier had to&nbsp;
carefully ponder what projects to sign on to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:17.600
Shockingly enough, this luminary of the&nbsp;
cinema chose unwisely twice in a row.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:22.160
He received his first Razzie for Worst&nbsp;
Supporting Actor thanks to an ill-advised remake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:25.120
of The Jazz Singer that also starred Neil Diamond.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:29.440
As for Olivier's second Razzie, war&nbsp;
movies often do well at the box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:32.880
and on the awards circuit. They come&nbsp;
with a built-in gravitas, after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:37.040
thanks to their subject matter and high&nbsp;
stakes. But that didn't prevent Olivier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:41.840
from being named Worst Actor for Inchon!,&nbsp;
a Korean War epic that has a zero percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:46.761
Rotten Tomatoes score and is sometimes&nbsp;
cited as the worst war movie ever made.

